.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt WBINDKEYS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm wbindkeys
.Nd bind keys, mouse buttons and scroll events to commands on Wayland
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Nm
.Cm permissions
.Fl -check | -set
.Nm
.Cm man
.Op Fl -install
.Nm
.Fl h | V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs shell commands when key combinations, mouse buttons or scroll events
are pressed.
It is a Wayland replacement for
.Xr xbindkeys 1 ,
configured with a Lua script.
.Pp
Input is read directly from the input devices in
.Pa /dev/input
through libinput rather than through the compositor, so it works the same
under any Wayland compositor.
Because of this, the user running
.Nm
needs read access to those devices; see
.Sx PERMISSIONS
below.
.Pp
Run without a subcommand,
.Nm
loads its configuration and keeps running, listening for input.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d , Fl -debug
Print log output to stderr: which input devices were opened (or could not
be opened), the configuration file being loaded, each registered binding and
the key codes it maps to, unrecognised key names, every key press with its
combination, and whether it matched a binding.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a summary of options and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm permissions Fl -check
Check that the udev rule is installed and that every input device can be
read by the current user.
Run it as your normal user, not as root.
Lists any devices that cannot be read and exits with status 1 if anything is
wrong.
.It Cm permissions Fl -set
Install the udev rule in
.Pa /etc/udev/rules.d/69-wbindkeys.rules ,
reload udev rules and re-trigger input devices so the current session gets
access.
Must be run as root.
.It Cm man Op Fl -install
Print this manual page.
With
.Fl -install ,
write it to
.Pa ../share/man/man1/wbindkeys.1
relative to the
.Nm
binary instead, e.g.\&
.Pa ~/.cargo/share/man/man1
after
.Ic cargo install
or
.Pa ~/.local/share/man/man1
after
.Ic make install .
.Xr man 1
looks there automatically for programs in
.Pa ~/.cargo/bin
or
.Pa ~/.local/bin
on your
.Ev PATH ,
so no
.Ev MANPATH
changes are needed.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a Lua script.
Bindings are created with the
.Fn bind
function, which maps a key combination to a shell command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bind("ALT+T", "alacritty")
bind("MOD+Mouse4", "wofi --show drun")
bind("ALT+ScrollUp", "pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ +5%")
.Ed
.Pp
Commands are run with
.Xr sh 1
and their output is discarded.
.Pp
Key names are case-insensitive and joined with
.Sq + .
The available names are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Modifiers
ALT, CTRL, SHIFT, MOD (the Super key), SPACE, and the right-hand
RIGHTALT, RIGHTCTRL, RIGHTSHIFT, RIGHTMOD.
ALT, CTRL, SHIFT and MOD refer to the left-hand keys.
.It Letters, numbers and function keys
A to Z, 0 to 9, F1 to F12.
.It Navigation
UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, HOME, END, PAGEUP, PAGEDOWN, INSERT, DELETE.
.It Other keys
ESCAPE, ENTER, BACKSPACE, TAB, BACKTICK, COMMA, PERIOD, SLASH, BACKSLASH,
SEMICOLON, QUOTE, DASH, EQUAL, LEFTSQUARE, RIGHTSQUARE.
.It Mouse
MOUSE1 to MOUSE10, SCROLLUP, SCROLLDOWN, SCROLLLEFT, SCROLLRIGHT.
.El
.Pp
When a binding has several modifiers they must be pressed in the order they
are written.
Unknown key names are ignored; run with
.Fl -debug
to see them.
.Sh PERMISSIONS
.Nm
runs as a normal user and needs read access to
.Pa /dev/input .
Running
.Ic sudo wbindkeys permissions --set
once installs a udev rule that tags input devices with
.Cm uaccess ,
so
.Xr systemd-logind 8
grants access to the user in the active local session.
Check access with
.Ic wbindkeys permissions --check .
.Pp
If devices are still not readable, re-run
.Cm permissions Fl -set ,
or log out and back in.
Access is only granted to the session at the physical seat, so it does not
work over SSH.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory the configuration file is read from; see
.Sx FILES .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wbindkeys/init.lua
Configuration file.
Defaults to
.Pa ~/.config/wbindkeys/init.lua
when
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set.
.Nm
exits with an error if it does not exist.
.It Pa /etc/udev/rules.d/69-wbindkeys.rules
udev rule installed by
.Cm permissions Fl -set .
.It Pa ~/.config/systemd/user/wbindkeys.service
systemd user service created by
.Ic make install .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success, or
.Cm permissions Fl -check
found no problems.
.It 1
.Cm permissions Fl -check
found a problem,
.Cm permissions Fl -set
failed, or
.Cm man Fl -install
could not write the page.
.It 2
Invalid command line arguments.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Debug a configuration (stop the service first so it doesn't also react):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
systemctl --user stop wbindkeys.service
wbindkeys --debug
.Ed
.Pp
Grant access to input devices when
.Nm
is in
.Pa ~/.local/bin :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo ~/.local/bin/wbindkeys permissions --set
.Ed
.Pp
Check access:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wbindkeys permissions --check
.Ed
.Pp
Install this manual page after
.Ic cargo install wbindkeys :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wbindkeys man --install
man wbindkeys
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xbindkeys 1 ,
.Xr libinput 4 ,
.Xr udev 7 ,
.Xr systemd-logind 8
